statement:
A robot stands at `(0, 0)` on an unbounded grid, facing north. North is the `+y` direction, east `+x`, south `-y` and west `-x`. It carries out `commands` in order:

- `-2`: turn 90 degrees to the left;
- `-1`: turn 90 degrees to the right;
- `k` with `1 <= k <= 9`: walk forward `k` unit steps, one step at a time.

Each `obstacles[i] = [x_i, y_i]` blocks one grid point. If the next step of a walk would land on an obstacle, the robot stays put and drops the rest of that command, then continues with the next one. An obstacle may sit at `(0, 0)`: it does not affect the robot while it stands at the start, but the robot can never step back onto the origin.

Return the largest squared distance `x * x + y * y` from the origin over every point the robot occupies during the whole run.

validator:
function validate([commands, obstacles]) {
  if (commands.length < 1 || commands.length > 10000) return 'commands needs 1 to 10^4 entries.';
  if (commands.some(c => !Number.isInteger(c) || !(c === -2 || c === -1 || (c >= 1 && c <= 9)))) return 'Each command is -2, -1, or an integer from 1 to 9.';
  if (obstacles.length > 10000) return 'There are at most 10^4 obstacles.';
  if (obstacles.some(p => p.length !== 2 || p.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < -30000 || v > 30000))) return 'Each obstacle is [x, y] with coordinates from -3 * 10^4 to 3 * 10^4.';
  const units = commands.reduce((sum, c) => sum + (c > 0 ? c : 0), 0);
  if (units > 46340) return 'The robot may walk too far for the answer to stay below 2^31.';
  return null;
}

